WHY WE ASK

A Nomen deployment is never one person's work. It runs on PHP and a database, it
links libraries, it loads plugins and skins, and it stands on the packages those
bring with them. Each of those pieces has authors, a license and a notice of its
own.

The Version Page is where a deployment names them. It is not a monument to the
author of the Program. It is the place where a site shows, in a single view,
whose work it is standing on: the Program, the runtime, the database, the
libraries, the plugins, the skins. That is why we ask you to keep it.


WHAT WE ASK

1. Keep the notices.

The Program carries a copyright notice and a notice that it is licensed under
GPLv3. When you convey the Program or a modified version, GPLv3 already requires
you to keep them; that requirement comes from GPLv3, not from this file. When
you are only running a deployment, we ask you to keep them too, because they
tell visitors what the software is.

The footer credit is optional. To remove it, set 'powered_by' => false in
config/brand.php; the rest of the page is unchanged.

2. Keep the Version Page.

The page at /special/version lists the Program, PHP, the database, the
libraries, the plugins and the skins. We ask you to keep it publicly reachable.
It is useful to visitors, fair to the authors, and the simplest answer to "what
is this site running?".

Before you change it, check the licenses of the plugins and skins you installed:
some of them require their notices to be shown, and the Version Page is where
those notices live. That obligation comes from their licenses, not from Nomen.

3. Keep the name for the Program.

We are happy for an unmodified copy of Nomen to be called Nomen. If you modify
the Program, we ask you to give the result its own name: a fork called Nomen is
confusing for users and unfair to the original. You may still say truthfully
that it is based on Nomen or powered by it.

The name and the logo are not licensed by GPLv3. This paragraph is a request,
not a trademark license; nothing here conditions your rights under GPLv3.

4. Check the licenses of what you install.

Plugins and skins are other people's work, under their own licenses. Nothing in
this file can waive or change those licenses, and we cannot waive them on their
authors' behalf. If you install a plugin or a skin, read its license; if you
convey it, keep its notices. This is a reminder, not a request: those licenses
bind you on their own terms.

5. When you convey, GPLv3 applies in full.

Running a deployment and conveying the Program are different things. If you
convey the Program or a modified version, GPLv3 applies in full: source code,
copyright and license notices, and marking of modifications. Renaming a
deployment does not change that and does not suspend GPLv3.


